)TESTS
AID TOKEN STRING-LITERALS
ATITLE CDATA String Literals
(TESTS
(PROSE
-String literals are enclosed in double quotes. They may span multiple lines, with line breaks preserved in the resulting string.\n        
)PROSE
AID TOKEN STRING-HELLO
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM IMPLIED
(TEST
(INPUT
-"hello"
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-hello\n        
)EXPECT
)TEST
AID TOKEN STRING-EMPTY
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM IMPLIED
(TEST
(INPUT
-""
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-\n    
)EXPECT
)TEST
)TESTS
AID TOKEN CHARACTER-LITERALS
ATITLE CDATA Character Literals
(TESTS
(PROSE
-Character literals represent a single character, enclosed in backticks.\n        
)PROSE
AID TOKEN CHAR-A
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM IMPLIED
(TEST
(INPUT
-`a`
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-a\n        
)EXPECT
)TEST
AID TOKEN CHAR-Z
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM IMPLIED
(TEST
(INPUT
-`Z`
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-Z\n
)EXPECT
)TEST
)TESTS
)CATEGORY
AID TOKEN OPERATIONS
ATITLE CDATA Operations
(CATEGORY
(PROSE
-Operations allow combining expressions.\n\n    
)PROSE
AID TOKEN PREFIX-FUNCTIONS
ATITLE CDATA Prefix Functions
(TESTS
(PROSE
-An operation is a sequence of operands that evaluates to a single value.\n        Each operand is an expression optionally followed by member access. An operation ends with a colon, a line break,\n        or anything which is not an expression. Evaluation uses a context which is empty at the start of an operation.\n        When the context is empty, operator-shaped identifiers act as prefix functions, taking the next operand as their argument.\n        
)PROSE
AID TOKEN NEGATE-POSITIVE
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM IMPLIED
(TEST
(INPUT
--7
)INPUT
(EXPECT
--7\n        
)EXPECT
)TEST
AID TOKEN NEGATE-NEGATIVE
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM IMPLIED
(TEST
(INPUT
--(-3)
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-3\n        
)EXPECT
)TEST
AID TOKEN PREFIX-ADD
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM IMPLIED
(TEST
(INPUT
-+ 3
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-3\n\n    
)EXPECT
)TEST
)TESTS
AID TOKEN OPERATOR-CHAINS
ATITLE CDATA Operator Chains
(TESTS
(PROSE
-When the context holds a value, operators combine with operands according to standard mathematical precedence.\n        Multiplicative operators (*, /, %, div, mod) bind tighter than additive operators (+, -).\n        Comparison and equality operators have lower precedence than arithmetic.\n        Operators of equal precedence associate left-to-right.\n        
)PROSE
AID TOKEN SIMPLE-ADD
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM IMPLIED
(TEST
(INPUT
-3 + 4
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-7\n        
)EXPECT
)TEST
AID TOKEN SIMPLE-SUBTRACT
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM IMPLIED
(TEST
(INPUT
-10 - 3
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-7\n        
)EXPECT
)TEST
AID TOKEN SIMPLE-MULTIPLY
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM IMPLIED
(TEST
(INPUT
-6 * 7
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-42\n        
)EXPECT
)TEST
AID TOKEN SIMPLE-DIVIDE
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM IMPLIED
(TEST
(INPUT
-84 / 2
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-42\n        
)EXPECT
)TEST
AID TOKEN SIMPLE-MODULO
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM IMPLIED
(TEST
(INPUT
-17 % 5
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-2\n        
)EXPECT
)TEST
AID TOKEN CHAIN-WITH-PRECEDENCE
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM IMPLIED
(TEST
(INPUT
-3 + 4 * 2
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-11\n        
)EXPECT
)TEST
AID TOKEN CHAIN-LEFT-TO-RIGHT
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM IMPLIED
(TEST
(INPUT
-20 - 5 - 3
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-12\n\n    
)EXPECT
)TEST
)TESTS
AID TOKEN PARENTHESES-GROUPING
ATITLE CDATA Parentheses and Grouping
(TESTS
(PROSE
-Parentheses create a empty context. This allows prefix forms inside an otherwise operator chain,\n        and explicit grouping when needed.\n        
)PROSE
AID TOKEN PARENS-NEGATE
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM IMPLIED
(TEST
(INPUT
-5 * (- 2)
)INPUT
(EXPECT
--10\n        
)EXPECT
)TEST
AID TOKEN PARENS-GROUPING
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM IMPLIED
(TEST
(INPUT
-2 * (3 + 4)
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-14\n        
)EXPECT
)TEST
AID TOKEN NESTED-PARENS
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM IMPLIED
(TEST
(INPUT
-(2 + 3) * (4 + 1)
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-25\n\n    
)EXPECT
)TEST
)TESTS
AID TOKEN IDENTIFIER-SHAPES
ATITLE CDATA Identifier Shapes
(TESTS
(PROSE
-Identifiers can have name shape (alphanumeric) or operator shape (symbols). The shape is purely lexical and does not determine behavior. Whether an identifier acts as a prefix function or chain operator depends on its declaration and the register state.\n        
)PROSE
AID TOKEN NAME-AS-OPERATOR
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM IMPLIED
(TEST
(INPUT
-10 div 3
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-3\n        
)EXPECT
)TEST
AID TOKEN NAME-AS-FUNCTION
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM IMPLIED
(TEST
(INPUT
-abs (- 5)
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-5\n\n    
)EXPECT
)TEST
)TESTS
AID TOKEN LET-BINDINGS
ATITLE CDATA Let Bindings
(TESTS
(PROSE
-Let bindings introduce local variables. The syntax is let followed by a name and a value expression (no equals sign). Multiple statements are separated by colons or newlines.\n        
)PROSE
AID TOKEN LET-SIMPLE
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM TOKEN PROGRAM
(TEST
(INPUT
-let x 5\nx
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-5\n        
)EXPECT
)TEST
AID TOKEN LET-USE-BINDING
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM TOKEN PROGRAM
(TEST
(INPUT
-let x 5\nx + 3
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-8\n        
)EXPECT
)TEST
AID TOKEN LET-NESTED
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM TOKEN PROGRAM
(TEST
(INPUT
-let x 5\nlet y 3\nx + y
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-8\n        
)EXPECT
)TEST
AID TOKEN LET-EXPR-VALUE
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM TOKEN PROGRAM
(TEST
(INPUT
-let x 2 + 3\nx * 2
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-10\n
)EXPECT
)TEST
)TESTS
)CATEGORY
AID TOKEN COMPARISON-OPERATORS
ATITLE CDATA Comparison Operators
(CATEGORY
(PROSE
-Comparison operators compare two values and return a boolean result.\n\n    
)PROSE
AID TOKEN EQUALITY
ATITLE CDATA Equality
(TESTS
(PROSE
-The equality operator (=) tests if two values are equal. The inequality operator (not equal to) tests if two values are different.\n        
)PROSE
AID TOKEN EQUAL-TRUE
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM IMPLIED
(TEST
(INPUT
-5 = 5
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-true\n        
)EXPECT
)TEST
AID TOKEN EQUAL-FALSE
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM IMPLIED
(TEST
(INPUT
-5 = 3
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-false\n        
)EXPECT
)TEST
AID TOKEN NOT-EQUAL-TRUE
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM IMPLIED
(TEST
(INPUT
-5 <> 3
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-true\n        
)EXPECT
)TEST
AID TOKEN NOT-EQUAL-FALSE
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM IMPLIED
(TEST
(INPUT
-5 <> 5
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-false\n\n    
)EXPECT
)TEST
)TESTS
AID TOKEN RELATIONAL
ATITLE CDATA Relational Operators
(TESTS
(PROSE
-Relational operators compare the ordering of values.\n        
)PROSE
AID TOKEN LESS-THAN-TRUE
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM IMPLIED
(TEST
(INPUT
-3 < 5
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-true\n        
)EXPECT
)TEST
AID TOKEN LESS-THAN-FALSE
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM IMPLIED
(TEST
(INPUT
-5 < 3
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-false\n        
)EXPECT
)TEST
AID TOKEN GREATER-THAN-TRUE
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM IMPLIED
(TEST
(INPUT
-5 > 3
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-true\n        
)EXPECT
)TEST
AID TOKEN GREATER-THAN-FALSE
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM IMPLIED
(TEST
(INPUT
-3 > 5
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-false\n        
)EXPECT
)TEST
AID TOKEN LESS-OR-EQUAL-TRUE
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM IMPLIED
(TEST
(INPUT
-5 <= 5
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-true\n        
)EXPECT
)TEST
AID TOKEN LESS-OR-EQUAL-FALSE
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM IMPLIED
(TEST
(INPUT
-5 <= 3
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-false\n        
)EXPECT
)TEST
AID TOKEN GREATER-OR-EQUAL-TRUE
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM IMPLIED
(TEST
(INPUT
-5 >= 5
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-true\n        
)EXPECT
)TEST
AID TOKEN GREATER-OR-EQUAL-FALSE
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM IMPLIED
(TEST
(INPUT
-3 >= 5
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-false\n
)EXPECT
)TEST
)TESTS
)CATEGORY
AID TOKEN BOOLEAN-OPERATORS
ATITLE CDATA Boolean Operators
(CATEGORY
(PROSE
-Boolean operators combine boolean values.\n\n    
)PROSE
AID TOKEN BOOLEAN-NEGATION
ATITLE CDATA Boolean Negation
(TESTS
(PROSE
-The tilde operator negates a boolean value.\n        
)PROSE
AID TOKEN NEGATE-TRUE
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM IMPLIED
(TEST
(INPUT
-~true
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-false\n        
)EXPECT
)TEST
AID TOKEN NEGATE-FALSE
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM IMPLIED
(TEST
(INPUT
-~false
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-true\n\n    
)EXPECT
)TEST
)TESTS
AID TOKEN BOOLEAN-AND-OR
ATITLE CDATA Boolean And/Or
(TESTS
(PROSE
-The ampersand operator performs logical AND, the pipe operator performs logical OR.\n        
)PROSE
AID TOKEN AND-TRUE-TRUE
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM IMPLIED
(TEST
(INPUT
-true & true
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-true\n        
)EXPECT
)TEST
AID TOKEN AND-TRUE-FALSE
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM IMPLIED
(TEST
(INPUT
-true & false
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-false\n        
)EXPECT
)TEST
AID TOKEN OR-FALSE-FALSE
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM IMPLIED
(TEST
(INPUT
-false | false
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-false\n        
)EXPECT
)TEST
AID TOKEN OR-TRUE-FALSE
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM IMPLIED
(TEST
(INPUT
-true | false
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-true\n
)EXPECT
)TEST
)TESTS
)CATEGORY
AID TOKEN BITWISE-OPERATORS
ATITLE CDATA Bitwise Operators
(CATEGORY
(PROSE
-Bitwise operators manipulate integer values at the bit level.\n\n    
)PROSE
AID TOKEN BIT-SHIFTS
ATITLE CDATA Bit Shifts
(TESTS
(PROSE
-The left shift operator shifts bits to the left, effectively multiplying by powers of two. The right shift operator shifts bits to the right, effectively dividing by powers of two.\n        
)PROSE
AID TOKEN LEFT-SHIFT-ONE
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM IMPLIED
(TEST
(INPUT
-1 << 4
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-16\n        
)EXPECT
)TEST
AID TOKEN LEFT-SHIFT-FIVE
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM IMPLIED
(TEST
(INPUT
-5 << 3
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-40\n        
)EXPECT
)TEST
AID TOKEN RIGHT-SHIFT-SIXTEEN
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM IMPLIED
(TEST
(INPUT
-16 >> 2
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-4\n        
)EXPECT
)TEST
AID TOKEN RIGHT-SHIFT-HUNDRED
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM IMPLIED
(TEST
(INPUT
-100 >> 3
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-12\n
)EXPECT
)TEST
)TESTS
)CATEGORY
AID TOKEN LAMBDAS
ATITLE CDATA Lambdas
(CATEGORY
(PROSE
-A lambda is an anonymous function written with a backslash:\n    `\\params: body`. Parameters are space-separated. A lambda bound to a name\n    and applied to arguments is monomorphized at the call site for those\n    argument types, so the same lambda works at any argument type.\n\n    
)PROSE
AID TOKEN LAMBDA-APPLICATION
ATITLE CDATA Lambda Application
(TESTS
(PROSE
-A lambda bound to a name can be called like a function.\n        
)PROSE
AID TOKEN LAMBDA-INC
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM TOKEN PROGRAM
(TEST
(INPUT
-let inc \\x: x + 1\ninc(5)
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-6\n        
)EXPECT
)TEST
AID TOKEN LAMBDA-ADD
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM TOKEN PROGRAM
(TEST
(INPUT
-let add \\x y: x + y\nadd(3, 4)
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-7\n        
)EXPECT
)TEST
AID TOKEN LAMBDA-REUSED
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM TOKEN PROGRAM
(TEST
(INPUT
-let dbl \\x: x + x\nlet a dbl(10)\nlet b dbl(21)\na + b
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-62\n\n    
)EXPECT
)TEST
)TESTS
AID TOKEN LAMBDA-CAPTURES
ATITLE CDATA Captures
(TESTS
(PROSE
-A lambda captures enclosing variables by value. The captured\n        value is fixed when the lambda is applied.\n        
)PROSE
AID TOKEN LAMBDA-CAPTURE-ONE
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM TOKEN PROGRAM
(TEST
(INPUT
-let n 10\nlet f \\x: x + n\nf(5)
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-15\n        
)EXPECT
)TEST
AID TOKEN LAMBDA-CAPTURE-MANY
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM TOKEN PROGRAM
(TEST
(INPUT
-let a 3\nlet b 4\nlet g \\x: x + a + b\ng(10)
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-17\n\n    
)EXPECT
)TEST
)TESTS
AID TOKEN LAMBDA-BLOCK-CAPTURES
ATITLE CDATA Captures in Braced Bodies
(TESTS
(PROSE
-Capture discovery reaches inside a braced body, and a body-local\n        binding that shadows an enclosing name is the local, not a capture.\n        
)PROSE
AID TOKEN LAMBDA-CAPTURE-BLOCK
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM TOKEN PROGRAM
(TEST
(INPUT
-let n 10\nlet cap \\x: { x + n }\ncap(5)
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-15\n        
)EXPECT
)TEST
AID TOKEN LAMBDA-CAPTURE-SHADOW
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM TOKEN PROGRAM
(TEST
(INPUT
-let n 10\nlet shade \\x: { let n 99\nx + n }\nshade(5)
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-104\n\n    
)EXPECT
)TEST
)TESTS
AID TOKEN LAMBDA-EMBEDDED
ATITLE CDATA Embedded Calls
(TESTS
(PROSE
-A lambda call may appear anywhere inside a larger expression, not\n        only as the whole right-hand side of a binding. Each call is resolved to\n        a direct call before operator precedence is applied.\n        
)PROSE
AID TOKEN LAMBDA-EMBED-SUM
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM TOKEN PROGRAM
(TEST
(INPUT
-let inc \\x: x + 1\nlet add \\x y: x + y\ninc(5) + add(3, 4)
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-13\n        
)EXPECT
)TEST
AID TOKEN LAMBDA-EMBED-MIX
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM TOKEN PROGRAM
(TEST
(INPUT
-let inc \\x: x + 1\nlet n 100\nn + inc(9)
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-110\n        
)EXPECT
)TEST
AID TOKEN LAMBDA-EMBED-CAPTURE
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM TOKEN PROGRAM
(TEST
(INPUT
-let n 100\nlet cap \\x: x + n\ncap(1) + cap(2)
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-203\n\n    
)EXPECT
)TEST
)TESTS
AID TOKEN LAMBDA-DELEGATES
ATITLE CDATA Delegate Values
(TESTS
(PROSE
-A `delegate` names a function type. A non-capturing lambda assigned\n        to a delegate-typed binding is a function-pointer value that can be passed\n        to a function and called indirectly.\n        
)PROSE
AID TOKEN DELEGATE-APPLY
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM TOKEN PROGRAM
(TEST
(INPUT
-define IntFn delegate(int) returns int\nfunction apply(f: IntFn, x: int) returns int\n    f(x)\nlet g: IntFn \\x: x + 1\napply(g, 5)
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-6\n        
)EXPECT
)TEST
AID TOKEN DELEGATE-TWICE
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM TOKEN PROGRAM
(TEST
(INPUT
-define IntFn delegate(int) returns int\nfunction twice(f: IntFn, x: int) returns int\n    f(f(x))\nlet dbl: IntFn \\x: x + x\ntwice(dbl, 20)
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-80\n\n    
)EXPECT
)TEST
)TESTS
AID TOKEN LAMBDA-CLOSURES
ATITLE CDATA Closures
(TESTS
(PROSE
-A capturing lambda assigned to a delegate type is a true closure\n        value carrying the captured variables. (Closures returned from a\n        function escape onto the caller's region -- see the escaping-closure AOT\n        regression test.)\n        
)PROSE
AID TOKEN CLOSURE-CAPTURE
ASKIP IMPLIED
APROGRAM TOKEN PROGRAM
(TEST
(INPUT
-define IntFn delegate(int) returns int\nfunction apply(f: IntFn, x: int) returns int\n    f(x)\nlet n 10\nlet add: IntFn \\x: x + n\napply(add, 5)
)INPUT
(EXPECT
-15\n
)EXPECT
)TEST
)TESTS
)CATEGORY
)SUITE
C
